#
# One clause that passes today and is documented to change in a later release. It passes, so the
# deprecation notice is the only observable output.
#
# `Name` is a string and the list holds integers, so nothing in the list can be compared with it.
# `NOT IN` reads that as "not a member" and passes, while `!=` on the same operands fails closed.
#
# This file was `vacuous_and_incomparable_clauses.guard` and held a second clause, `Sizes == 50`
# over an empty `Sizes`, which passed without comparing anything and carried its own notice. That
# comparison now fails, so the clause left and the file took the name of the one clause it keeps;
# `empty_collection_comparisons.guard` covers the failure the other became.
#
rule incomparable_membership {
    Name not IN [10, 50, 100]
}
